# Line endings and binary handling.
#
# Without this file Git guesses, and the guess depends on each machine's
# core.autocrlf setting. That is why `git add` prints a wall of "LF will be
# replaced by CRLF" warnings on Windows. The code is edited on Windows but
# built on Linux in GitHub Actions, so the repository stores LF and each
# checkout gets whatever suits it.

# Detect text automatically, and always commit it with LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files where a stray CR would actually break something. GitHub Pages reads
# CNAME as a single hostname, and a trailing \r makes it a different hostname.
CNAME           text eol=lf
.nojekyll       text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Windows-only files keep CRLF so they open correctly in Notepad.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Binary. Never convert, never try to diff. Git usually infers these
# correctly, but 20 MB of photographs is not something to leave to a heuristic.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.webp          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
*.mp4           binary
*.zip           binary

# Generated. Collapse it in pull requests rather than reviewing 20k lines.
package-lock.json  -diff linguist-generated
